course: dictionary
lesson: 14739
title: Prominent: easy to see, or important
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Prominent has a few meanings, and the sentence tells you which one. When something sticks out so you notice it straight away, it is prominent. «The tall church tower is prominent on the hill, so you can see it from miles away.» The tower stands out, so prominent means easy to see.
explain: A person can be prominent too. «Maya is a prominent scientist, and her name is in many newspapers.» Here prominent means important and well known. The clue is that her name is in the newspapers, so lots of people know who she is.
explain: To work it out, look at the clues around the word. Ask: does this thing stand out, or is this person important? Then swap in an easy word like noticeable or famous and check that the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Prominent means easy to see because it stands out or sticks out.
fact: A prominent person is important and well known.
fact: Prominent can also mean important or noticeable in a situation.
fact: «A prominent place» means a spot where people will easily see something.
fact: «A prominent role» means an important part in a play, a team or a project.
exercise: «The poster was put in a prominent place by the door.» What does prominent mean here? | Very old and dusty | Made of paper | *Easy to see, standing out | Hidden in a dark corner
exercise: «The actor had a prominent role in the film.» What does prominent suggest? | A tiny part with one line | A part that nobody noticed | *An important part | A part that was cut out
exercise: «The prominent news reporter came to our school.» What does prominent mean here? | *Well known and important | Late for the bus | Very small | Angry with the children
test: «The prominent cliff could be seen from far out at sea.» What does prominent mean here? | Sold cheaply | Covered in snow | Sleepy and quiet | *Easy to see, standing out
test: Ben says prominent means shy. What does it really mean? | Very cold | *Easy to see, or important and well known | Lost and confused | Shy and quiet
exam: Which sentence uses prominent to mean important and well known? | A hot bowl of soup | A rainy day | *An author everyone knows | A sock lying under the bed
